        <h1 class="text-3xl sm:text-4xl font-extrabold text-slate-900 tracking-tight leading-tight">
          Bid Bonds vs. Performance Bonds: A Sub's Guide
        </h1>
        <AuthorByline />
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-slate-400">
          Published July 17, 2026 · Updated September 25, 2026 · 15 min read
        </p>
      </div>

      <div
        class="prose prose-slate max-w-none prose-headings:font-semibold prose-headings:tracking-tight prose-a:text-brand-600"
      >
        <p>
          <strong>
            A bid bond guarantees that you'll sign the contract at the price you bid; a performance
            bond guarantees that you'll actually finish the work once you have signed.
          </strong> The bid bond covers the bidding stage and usually costs little or nothing; the performance
          bond (almost always paired with a payment bond) covers the whole job and carries a premium,
          typically somewhere around 0.5–3% of the contract value.
        </p>
        <p>
          This guide is written for specialty subcontractors — the electrical, mechanical, framing,
          glazing and concrete shops that get handed a bond requirement in a bid invitation or a
          subcontract and need to know what they're agreeing to. Construction bonds aren't
          complicated once you understand what each one guarantees and who it protects, but they
          have real consequences for how you price work and manage your cash.
        </p>
        <p>
          Below: the three contract bonds subs encounter — bid, performance and payment — what each
          one costs, what the alternatives to a bid bond are, when you need bonds at all, and what
          it takes to get bonded.
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-xl bg-slate-50 border border-slate-100 p-6 not-prose my-8">
          <h2 class="text-base font-semibold text-slate-900 mb-3">Key takeaways</h2>
          <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-600 list-disc pl-5">
            <li>
              A bid bond guarantees you'll honor your price if selected — the surety covers the
              owner's re-bid cost if you walk away.
            </li>
            <li>
              A performance bond guarantees you'll complete the work; a payment bond guarantees
              you'll pay your workers and suppliers. They're almost always required together.
            </li>
            <li>
              Federal public work over $150,000 requires P&amp;P bonds (Miller Act); most states
              have equivalent statutes. GCs often flow requirements down to major subs.
            </li>
            <li>
              Surety capacity is earned over time through clean financials, credit, and a track
              record of completed projects. Build the relationship before you need the bond.
            </li>
            <li>
              Bid bonds are usually issued free or for a small fee by a surety that expects to write
              your performance bond. P&amp;P premiums typically run about 0.5–3% of contract value,
              on a sliding scale — factor them in before you finalize your bid numbers.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2>What is a bid bond?</h2>
        <p>
          A bid bond is a guarantee — backed by a surety company — that you will honor your bid
          price and execute a contract if you are selected as the low bidder. If you win the job and
          then refuse to sign at the price you submitted, the surety compensates the owner for the
          difference between your bid and the next lowest bid, up to the bond penalty amount.
        </p>
        <p>
          The bond amount (the "penalty" or "penal sum") is typically 5–10% of the bid amount on
          state, local and private work, though some agencies specify a fixed dollar figure. Federal
          work is higher: under FAR 28.101-2, a federal bid guarantee must be at least 20% of the
          bid price, capped at $3 million. Whatever the figure, it's written in the bid invitation.
        </p>
        <p>
          The practical effect of a bid bond is simple: it screens out bidders who are fishing for
          pricing information or who haven't done the homework to know whether they can actually
          build the job at the number they submitted. Owners and general contractors use them as a
          threshold filter on public projects and larger commercial contracts.
        </p>

        <h3>Who are the parties to a bid bond?</h3>
        <p>A bid bond — like every contract surety bond — has three parties:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Principal</strong> — you, the bidder whose promise is being guaranteed.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Obligee</strong> — the party the bond protects: the project owner, or the GC when
            a sub is bidding to a general contractor.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Surety</strong> — the bonding company that backs your promise and pays the obligee
            if you default.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Unlike insurance, the surety expects to be paid back. Before issuing any bond you'll sign
          a general indemnity agreement — often with personal guarantees from the company's owners —
          so if the surety pays a claim, it can recover that money from you.
        </p>

        <h2>How much does a bid bond cost?</h2>
        <p>
          For a contractor with an established surety relationship, a bid bond is typically free or
          carries a small flat fee. The surety isn't earning its money on the bid bond; it earns it
          on the performance and payment bond premium you'll pay if you win the job. That's why most
          sureties only issue bid bonds to contractors they've already prequalified for the final
          bonds — the bid bond is effectively a commitment to write the P&amp;P bond at that size.
        </p>
        <p>
          Contractors without an established program sometimes pay a small premium for a standalone
          bid bond, and the price depends on the same credit and financial factors that set
          performance bond rates. If a broker quotes you a meaningful fee for a bid bond, ask
          whether that surety will also write the performance bond — if it won't, the bid bond
          doesn't do you much good.
        </p>

        <h3>Are bid bonds low risk for sureties?</h3>
        <p>
          Relatively, yes — which is the other reason they're cheap. A bid bond is short-term (it
          expires once the contract is signed or the bid is rejected), the surety has already
          underwritten the contractor before issuing it, and the exposure is capped at the
          difference between your bid and the next bidder, up to the penal sum — not the cost of
          finishing a whole project. Contractors who honor their bids never trigger a claim. The
          real risk starts with the performance bond, which is why that's where the underwriting and
          the premium sit.
        </p>

        <h3>Alternatives to a bid bond</h3>
        <p>
          Most public procurement rules accept other forms of bid security. On federal work, FAR
          28.204 allows these in lieu of a surety bond; state and local rules vary, so check what
          the bid invitation allows:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Certified or cashier's check</strong> for the full bid-security amount. Simple, but
            it ties up your cash from bid day until the award is made or the bids are rejected.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Irrevocable letter of credit</strong> from your bank. It doesn't take cash out of
            your account, but it counts against your credit line and banks charge a fee for it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Cash deposit or government securities</strong> (U.S. bonds or notes on federal work)
            held by the owner as security.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          For a sub who bids regularly, the math favors a surety bid bond: it costs little or
          nothing and doesn't lock up working capital. The alternatives make sense for an occasional
          small bid, or when you're still building a surety relationship.
        </p>

        <h2>What is a performance bond?</h2>
        <p>
          A performance bond guarantees that you will complete the contracted work according to the
          plans, specifications, and schedule. If you default — whether because you abandon the
          project, go out of business, or fail to meet contract requirements — the surety steps in.
          The surety has three options: hire a replacement contractor to finish the work, complete
          the work using its own forces, or pay the owner up to the bond amount (typically 100% of
          the contract value).
        </p>
        <p>
          From the owner's or GC's perspective, a performance bond converts your financial risk into
          the surety's risk. If you have a $500,000 subcontract with a performance bond, the owner
          knows that $500,000 of coverage stands behind your promise to finish the work.
        </p>

        <h2>What is a payment bond — and why it's almost always paired with performance</h2>
        <p>
          A payment bond guarantees that you will pay your laborers, sub-subcontractors, and
          material suppliers. It protects the parties downstream from you: the workers who can't
          file a mechanic's lien on public property, and the suppliers who might not get paid if you
          have a cash-flow crisis mid-project.
        </p>
        <p>
          Performance bonds and payment bonds are almost always required together — the combination
          is called a P&amp;P bond. They're two sides of the same obligation: completing the work
          and paying everyone involved. A project with a performance bond but no payment bond
          protects the owner from an unfinished building but leaves the <a
            href="/solutions/framing-contractors/">framing crew</a
          > or lumber yard with no recourse if money stops flowing. The Miller Act and state equivalents
          require both precisely because both protections are needed.
        </p>

